{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nAn errored side of a paired parity run is scored as a DECISION FLIP. Criterion (b) treats a flip as\nnever acceptable (\"it changes what ships\"), so a single transient throttle can fail the Bedrock slot\nas a model-quality regression.\n\nPROVEN BY EXPERIMENT through the real entry point, not by reading:\n20 gold pairs, both arms unanimous `block`, one v2 record replaced by the production errored record\n-> `flips=1, n_gold=20, passed=False`. Re-run end to end through the live driver\n`parallel_run_and_diff` (`parity.py:286-302`) with a runner raising `ThrottlingException` exactly\nonce: identical. One throttle trips four gating failures, including the never-acceptable criterion.\n\n## The mechanism\n\n`src/rebar/llm/parity.py:107`\n\n    decision_flips = sum(1 for a, b in gold_pairs if a.decision != b.decision)\n\ncompares `decision` on both sides of every gold-labelled pair with NO `errored` guard, while an\nerrored side carries a SYNTHETIC decision no model produced. `err1, err2` are computed at `:102` and\nunused here.\n\nThe sole production constructor of an errored record is\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/fidelity_spot_eval.py:174`:\n\n    return ItemRecord(valid=False, decision=\"dropped\", errored=True, label=label)\n\n`parity.py:46` documents the contract this violates: `errored` is \"a runtime error / timeout (NOT a\nmodel verdict)\". Criterion (d) already gates runtime error rate separately, so scoring an error under\n(b) both contradicts `:46` and double-counts one event.\n\n## Two things that look like the mechanism and are NOT — do not act on either\n\n1. It is NOT that the pair survives because `dropped` is a member of `_DECISIONS`. The filter at\n   `parity.py:105` is `if a.label in _DECISIONS` — it tests the GOLD LABEL, never `decision`.\n   `_DECISIONS` is doing double duty as both the decision vocabulary and the gold-label vocabulary,\n   which is what makes the line read as though it filters on decision. DISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT: an\n   errored side given `decision=\"__error__\"`, outside `_DECISIONS`, was still retained and counted\n   (`flips=1, passed=False`). NO value of `decision` can exempt an errored pair.\n2. It is therefore NOT fixable by changing what `fidelity_spot_eval.py:174` emits. That choice is\n   proximate but not load-bearing; the defect survives any value. A guard on decision-vocabulary\n   membership would be a NO-OP.\n\nThe changeable artifact is the missing `errored` guard at `parity.py:107`.\n\n## The silent case, which is worse than the loud one\n\nThe corroborating validity/error movement that might let a careful reader notice something is off\nexists ONLY because `fidelity_spot_eval.py:174` happens to couple `errored=True` with `valid=False`.\nNothing in `ItemRecord` enforces that pairing. A record with `errored=True, valid=True` yields\n`flips=1, validity_v2=1.0` — a COMPLETELY SILENT fabricated flip.\n\n## The report cannot distinguish fabricated flips from real ones\n\nThree v2 arms against the same v1, all reporting `decision_flips=3` — (A) 3 real / 0 errors,\n(B) 2 real + 1 error, (C) 0 real / 3 errors — produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL gating string. `metrics` has\nno per-pair detail and no invalid/excluded count; `error_rate` says an error occurred somewhere in\nthe arm, never which pairs. So a reader cannot discount fabricated flips even in principle.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An errored pair is excluded from `decision_flips` and reported as INVALID, with the guard at\n      `parity.py:107` reading `errored` rather than any decision-vocabulary membership test.\n- [ ] A test constructs a paired run where exactly one side errored and asserts it is treated as\n      INVALID rather than counted as a flip; it fails before the fix.\n- [ ] A test covers the `errored=True, valid=True` record specifically, since that is the silent\n      variant and the one no other signal catches.\n- [ ] The parity report distinguishes an errored/invalid pair from a genuine decision flip in its\n      output — a per-pair or excluded-count signal — so arms (A), (B) and (C) above are no longer\n      byte-identical.\n- [ ] make lint and make typecheck pass by exit code; the default selection is green."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785707082806390001,"uuid":"b0e1a87f-cd3e-438c-9c79-6a54c9685d7e"}